  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": "The findings are based on qualitative analysis of two specific case studies in Ho Chi Minh City and Mumbai, which may limit direct generalizability to cities with different administrative and legal structures.",
      "what": "This study analyzes bureaucratic practices of territorial securitization in Ho Chi Minh City and Mumbai, focusing on the disappearance of a ratified planning map in the Thፐ Thi\u0000m New Urban Area and the manipulation of eligibility lists in Mumbai's Slum Rehabilitation Scheme. It conceptualizes these actions as deliberate archival revisions that generate elastic, unstable categories of land and population rather than fixed property boundaries.",
      "why": "It demonstrates that the destabilization of official archives and property records is not a symptom of administrative failure or corruption, but rather a systematic, patterned technique of urban governance used to secure authority through the overproduction of bureaucratic instruments."
    }
